Harbor: mshtml: history.state, pushState() and replaceState() (#100).

Single-page applications give their screens addresses with history.pushState() and replaceState(), which
Internet Explorer has from version 10. mshtml's history object had neither, so a page's router failed with
"Object doesn't support this property or method" before it drew anything; Office's add-in Store page showed
its "Something went wrong" screen for that reason.

In IE10 mode and later the history object now has state, pushState(data, title, url) and
replaceState(data, title, url). The state is kept, and an address of the document's own origin becomes the
window's address, which is what location reports (another origin is refused). There is only ever one entry:
pushState() replaces as well, back() and forward() do what they did, and no popstate event is sent. Gecko
isn't told about the new address, so what a relative address is resolved against stays the address the
page was loaded from.

Test: WindowsTests/mshtml.

